obn for Debian/Ubuntu (arm64)
=============================

This package does not put the plugin where the slicer looks for it. It
can't: Bambu Studio and Orca Slicer load network plugins from the user's
own config directory, not from a system path.

What the package installs:

  /usr/lib/obn/            upstream's obn-linux-aarch64 release archive,
                           unmodified (install.sh, lib/vXX.XX.XX/*.so,
                           network_plugins.json per ABI, VERSION, README.txt)
  /usr/bin/obn-install     runs /usr/lib/obn/install.sh as you

What you do once per user, with the slicer closed:

  obn-install

That is upstream's documented install step. It asks which slicer, finds
its config dir (native ~/.config/BambuStudio, Flatpak
~/.var/app/com.bambulab.BambuStudio/config/BambuStudio, Bambu Studio Beta,
or Orca Slicer), reads the slicer version, picks the matching ABI build
from /usr/lib/obn/lib/, copies libbambu_networking.so and
libBambuSource.so into <config>/plugins/, installs ota/plugins/
network_plugins.json (Bambu Studio) and patches the slicer conf so it
stops re-downloading the stock plugin. It backs the conf up to
<conf>.obn-bak. Then start the slicer.

After upgrading this package, run obn-install again so the copy in your
config directory is updated too. If your slicer version is newer than
any lib/vXX.XX.XX/ directory in /usr/lib/obn, you need a newer upstream
release; see sources/obn/UPDATING.md in the asahi-debian-packages repo.

To go back to the stock plugin: delete <config>/plugins/libbambu_networking.so
and libBambuSource.so, restore <conf>.obn-bak, start the slicer and let it
download the plugin again.

Runtime settings live in <config>/obn.conf (created on first launch).
See upstream's README: https://github.com/ClusterM/open-bamboo-networking
